 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T16:45+08:00",
 "answer": "If by **KL** you mean **Kuala Lumpur, yes—stay indoors as much as you can today, Saturday, October 17**. At 4:00 p.m. local time, the Cheras monitoring station reported a Malaysian Air Pollutant Index of **164 (“Unhealthy”)**. Malaysia’s Ministry of Health advises people with asthma to avoid going out while the index is above 100. Conditions can vary across KL, so check the latest reading before any necessary trip. ([jerebumalaysia.com](https://jerebumalaysia.com/en/stations/cheras-w-p-kuala-lumpur/))\n\nKeep windows closed, use filtered air or an air purifier if available, and skip outdoor exercise. If you must go out, keep it brief; a well-fitting N95 may reduce particle exposure, but it can be uncomfortable for some people with asthma. Keep your reliever inhaler with you and follow your asthma action plan. ([epa.gov](https://www.epa.gov/wildfire-smoke-course/strategies-reduce-exposure-indoors?utm_source=openai))\n\nIf you’re **short of breath now**, use your reliever as directed in your action plan. Seek urgent care if symptoms don’t improve promptly; for severe breathing difficulty, call **999 in Malaysia**. ([nhlbi.nih.gov](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/asthma/attacks?utm_source=openai))",
